#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# mean

#% text
# `mean(xs)` gives the mean of the list of values `xs`, their sum divided by their number.

#% text
# `mean(d)` gives the mean of the distribution `d`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Exact values give an exact mean, machine reals a machine real, and symbols a symbolic expression.

#% item
# For a list of rows, such as points `[[x1, y1], [x2, y2], ...]`, the mean is taken column by column.

#% item
# The mean of a distribution is exact, and symbolic for symbolic parameters.

#% item
# An empty list is an error.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The mean of four integers:

#% cell
mean([1, 2, 3, 4])
#% output
#= 5/2

#% text
# Of machine reals:

#% cell
mean([1.5, 2.5, 4.0])
#% output
#= 2.6666666666666665

#% text
# Of symbols:

#% cell
mean([a, b, c])
#% output
#= (1/3)*(a + b + c)

#% text
# Of a distribution:

#% cell
mean(exponential_distribution(l))
#% output
#= l^-1

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Column by column, for a list of points:

#% cell
mean([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 9]])
#% output
#= [3, 5]

#% text
# Means of distributions with symbolic parameters:

#% cell
[mean(normal_distribution(mu, sigma)), mean(poisson_distribution(m)), mean(uniform_distribution([a, b]))]
#% output
#= [mu, m, (1/2)*(a + b)]

#% cell
mean(beta_distribution(2, 3))
#% output
#= 2/5

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The centre of a cloud of random points:

#% cell
seed(1); mean(random_variate(normal_distribution(3, 1), [1000, 2]))
#% output
#= [3.009304984244505, 2.9970135420853543]

#% text
# The mean of the points of a random walk started at [1, 1], as for the posterior mean of a Markov chain Monte Carlo run:

#% cell
seed(11); chain = nest_list(p => p + 0.1*random_variate(normal_distribution(), 2), [1, 1], 300); mean(chain)
#% output
#= [1.3359683668652962, 2.353046913944984]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The mean of 1, ..., n is (n + 1)/2:

#% cell
mean(range(100))
#% output
#= 101/2

#% text
# The sample mean approaches the mean of the distribution:

#% cell
seed(5); [mean(random_real([0, 1], 10^5)), mean(uniform_distribution([0, 1]))]
#% output
#= [0.5001847710444205, 1/2]

#% text
# The mean is sensitive to outliers, the median is not:

#% cell
[mean([1, 2, 3, 100]), median([1, 2, 3, 100])]
#% output
#= [53/2, 5/2]

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# The mean of an empty list:

#% cell
mean([])
#% output
#! failure(argument, mean, "the list is empty")

#% text
# A single number is not a list:

#% cell
mean(3)
#% output
#= mean(3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `median`, `variance`, `std_dev`, `quantile`, `total`, `expectation`
